[For "white"]
[Kind "main"]
[Start "ruy-main-b5 6.Bb3"]
[Name "...Bc5 and ...O-O: the e5 push"]

6... Bc5 { The most popular set-up at this level: Black's bishop takes the active diagonal and aims at f2. [%cal Rc5f2] }
7. c3 { The plan again. With d4 coming, the bishop on c5 will be hit by a pawn. [%cal Gc3d4] }
7... O-O
8. d4 { Hits the bishop and e5 at the same time. [%cal Gd4c5,Gd4e5] [%idea centre-pawns] }
8... exd4 { Nine players in ten take, and it hands White the whole centre. }
9. cxd4 { Two pawns side by side, and the bishop on c5 has to move. }
9... Bb6
( 9... Ba7? { Keeps the bishop on the diagonal, but it does nothing about e5. }
  10. e5 Nh5 { The knight goes to the edge. }
  11. Nc3 h6 12. g4 { The knight on h5 has nowhere to go, and it falls next move. White wins a knight. [%cal Gg4h5] [%idea trapped-piece] } )
10. e5 { Now the knight is hit. It has lost f6 for good, and every square it can go to is a poor one. [%cal Ge5f6] }
10... Ne8 { The usual reply. The knight goes home and blocks the rook. }
11. Bg5 { Develops with a hit on the queen. [%cal Gg5d8] }
11... Ne7 { Blocks the attack, with the knight from c6. }
12. Nc3 { White's pieces are out and working, while Black's are stuck behind each other. The engine has White more than three pawns ahead, though no material has changed: the gain is space and time. } *
